{
  "title": "Why Concorde Stopped Flying",
  "format": "explainer",
  "voice": "am_michael",
  "music": "calm",
  "palette": "midnight",
  "captions": true,
  "beats": [
    {
      "id": "b01",
      "narration": "There was a time when you could take off from London in the morning and land in New York earlier, by the local clock, than when you left.",
      "visual": {
        "template": "kinetic",
        "props": {
          "lines": [
            "Take off in London.",
            "Land in New York",
            "before you left."
          ],
          "emphasis": [
            "before"
          ]
        }
      },
      "sources": [
        "https://www.britishairways.com/content/information/about-ba/history-and-heritage/celebrating-concorde",
        "https://www.nms.ac.uk/explore-our-collections/stories/science-and-technology/concorde"
      ],
      "cues": [
        "take off",
        "land in New York",
        "earlier"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b02",
      "narration": "The crossing took about three and a half hours, roughly half the time of an ordinary jet. The aircraft that made it possible was Concorde.",
      "visual": {
        "template": "map_route",
        "props": {
          "from": {
            "name": "London",
            "lat": 51.47,
            "lon": -0.45
          },
          "to": {
            "name": "New York",
            "lat": 40.64,
            "lon": -73.78
          },
          "label": "London to New York: about 3 h 30 min"
        }
      },
      "sources": [
        "https://www.britishairways.com/content/information/about-ba/history-and-heritage/celebrating-concorde",
        "https://www.nms.ac.uk/explore-our-collections/stories/science-and-technology/concorde"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b03",
      "narration": "For twenty-seven years it was the fastest way for a passenger to cross an ocean. Then, in two thousand and three, it simply stopped. So what went wrong?",
      "visual": {
        "template": "title",
        "props": {
          "title": "Why Concorde Stopped Flying",
          "kicker": "Aviation history",
          "subtitle": "27 years at twice the speed of sound, and then it was gone"
        }
      },
      "sources": [
        "https://www.ebsco.com/research-starters/history/concorde-flies-passengers-supersonic-speeds",
        "https://www.history.com/this-day-in-history/october-24/the-concorde-makes-its-final-flight"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b04",
      "narration": "Concorde began with a treaty. In nineteen sixty-two, Britain and France agreed to build a supersonic airliner together and share the enormous cost.",
      "visual": {
        "template": "fact",
        "props": {
          "kicker": "29 November 1962",
          "text": "Britain and France sign a treaty to build a supersonic airliner together.",
          "source": "Anglo-French Concorde agreement"
        }
      },
      "sources": [
        "https://www.ebsco.com/research-starters/history/concorde-flies-passengers-supersonic-speeds",
        "https://www.gracesguide.co.uk/Concorde"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b05",
      "narration": "Assembly lines were set up in Filton, near Bristol, and in Toulouse. The first prototype took off from Toulouse in nineteen sixty-nine.",
      "visual": {
        "template": "map_route",
        "props": {
          "from": {
            "name": "Filton",
            "lat": 51.51,
            "lon": -2.58
          },
          "to": {
            "name": "Toulouse",
            "lat": 43.63,
            "lon": 1.37
          },
          "label": "Assembly lines: Filton and Toulouse",
          "vehicle": "none"
        }
      },
      "sources": [
        "https://www.ebsco.com/research-starters/history/concorde-flies-passengers-supersonic-speeds",
        "https://www.gracesguide.co.uk/Concorde"
      ],
      "cues": [
        "Filton"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b06",
      "narration": "In service, Concorde cruised at Mach two point oh four, more than twice the speed of sound, or about two thousand one hundred and eighty kilometres an hour.",
      "visual": {
        "template": "stat",
        "props": {
          "value": 2.04,
          "prefix": "Mach",
          "label": "Cruising speed: more than twice the speed of sound",
          "note": "about 2,180 km/h"
        }
      },
      "sources": [
        "https://www.britishairways.com/content/information/about-ba/history-and-heritage/celebrating-concorde",
        "https://www.gracesguide.co.uk/Concorde"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b07",
      "narration": "It flew as high as sixty thousand feet. From up there, passengers could see the curve of the Earth, under a sky turning dark blue.",
      "visual": {
        "template": "stat",
        "props": {
          "value": 60000,
          "suffix": "ft",
          "label": "Maximum cruising altitude",
          "note": "about 18 km, high enough to see the curve of the Earth"
        }
      },
      "sources": [
        "https://www.britishairways.com/content/information/about-ba/history-and-heritage/celebrating-concorde"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b08",
      "narration": "Air friction heated the skin so much that the nose reached about one hundred and twenty-seven degrees Celsius, and the whole airframe stretched by several inches in flight.",
      "visual": {
        "template": "kinetic",
        "props": {
          "lines": [
            "The nose reached 127°C.",
            "The airframe stretched",
            "by several inches."
          ],
          "emphasis": [
            "127°C",
            "stretched"
          ]
        }
      },
      "sources": [
        "https://www.gracesguide.co.uk/Concorde",
        "https://www.airport-technology.com/projects/concorde2/"
      ],
      "cues": [
        "the nose",
        "the whole airframe",
        "several inches"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b09",
      "narration": "Airlines were excited. In the nineteen sixties, sixteen carriers took out options on more than seventy aircraft. Supersonic travel looked like the future.",
      "visual": {
        "template": "fact",
        "props": {
          "kicker": "The order book",
          "text": "Sixteen airlines took out options on more than seventy Concordes.",
          "source": "Museums Victoria; Heritage Concorde"
        }
      },
      "sources": [
        "https://collections.museumsvictoria.com.au/articles/3544",
        "https://www.heritageconcorde.com/concorde-orders-and-options"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b10",
      "narration": "Then almost all of them walked away. Pan Am cancelled in early nineteen seventy-three and others followed, as the oil crisis made fuel expensive and the noise made Concorde unwelcome.",
      "visual": {
        "template": "list",
        "props": {
          "title": "Then the orders collapsed",
          "items": [
            "1973: Pan Am pulls out",
            "The oil crisis",
            "Noise and the sonic boom"
          ]
        }
      },
      "sources": [
        "https://www.heritageconcorde.com/concorde-orders-and-options",
        "https://www.ebsco.com/research-starters/history/concorde-flies-passengers-supersonic-speeds"
      ],
      "cues": [
        "Pan Am",
        "oil crisis",
        "noise"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b11",
      "narration": "In the end, only twenty Concordes were built, and just fourteen of them carried passengers: seven with British Airways and seven with Air France.",
      "visual": {
        "template": "stat",
        "props": {
          "value": 20,
          "label": "Concordes built",
          "note": "Only 14 flew for airlines: 7 with British Airways, 7 with Air France"
        }
      },
      "sources": [
        "https://www.ebsco.com/research-starters/history/concorde-flies-passengers-supersonic-speeds",
        "https://www.airport-technology.com/projects/concorde2/"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b12",
      "narration": "And there was a bigger problem: the sonic boom. In nineteen seventy-three, the United States banned civil supersonic flight over land, so Concorde could only fly fast over the sea.",
      "visual": {
        "template": "kinetic",
        "props": {
          "lines": [
            "Supersonic over land?",
            "Banned."
          ],
          "emphasis": [
            "Banned"
          ]
        }
      },
      "sources": [
        "https://reason.com/2016/07/26/how-the-faa-killed-supersonic-flight/"
      ],
      "cues": [
        "the sonic boom",
        "banned"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b13",
      "narration": "Passenger service began on the twenty-first of January, nineteen seventy-six. British Airways flew to Bahrain, while Air France flew to Rio de Janeiro.",
      "visual": {
        "template": "map_route",
        "props": {
          "from": {
            "name": "London",
            "lat": 51.47,
            "lon": -0.45
          },
          "to": {
            "name": "Bahrain",
            "lat": 26.27,
            "lon": 50.63
          },
          "label": "21 January 1976: BA to Bahrain"
        }
      },
      "sources": [
        "https://www.britishairways.com/content/information/about-ba/history-and-heritage/celebrating-concorde",
        "https://www.ebsco.com/research-starters/history/concorde-flies-passengers-supersonic-speeds"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b14",
      "narration": "But the numbers never really worked. Concorde carried about one hundred passengers. A Boeing jumbo jet carried around four hundred, and used far less fuel for every seat.",
      "visual": {
        "template": "comparison",
        "props": {
          "title": "Typical seats per flight",
          "unit": "seats",
          "left": {
            "label": "Concorde",
            "value": 100
          },
          "right": {
            "label": "Boeing 747",
            "value": 400
          }
        }
      },
      "sources": [
        "https://www.britishairways.com/content/information/about-ba/history-and-heritage/celebrating-concorde",
        "https://www.pbs.org/wgbh/nova/teachers/activities/3203_concorde.html"
      ],
      "cues": [
        "Concorde carried",
        "Boeing"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b15",
      "narration": "So tickets were expensive. In two thousand and three, a standard return ticket between London and New York cost about six thousand six hundred pounds.",
      "visual": {
        "template": "stat",
        "props": {
          "value": 6636,
          "prefix": "£",
          "label": "Standard London to New York return fare, 2003",
          "note": "National Museum of Flight"
        }
      },
      "sources": [
        "https://www.nms.ac.uk/explore-our-collections/stories/science-and-technology/concorde"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b16",
      "narration": "Its regulars were bankers, celebrities and executives, people for whom time was worth more than money. For British Airways, Concorde even made an operating profit for years.",
      "visual": {
        "template": "fact",
        "props": {
          "kicker": "Who flew Concorde",
          "text": "Bankers, celebrities and executives: people for whom time was worth more than money."
        }
      },
      "sources": [
        "https://www.csmonitor.com/1986/0122/oconc.html",
        "https://www.heritageconcorde.com/concorde-british-airways"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b17",
      "narration": "Then, on the twenty-fifth of July, two thousand, Air France Flight forty-five ninety began its take-off from Paris Charles de Gaulle, bound for New York.",
      "visual": {
        "template": "map_point",
        "props": {
          "place": {
            "name": "Paris-Charles de Gaulle",
            "lat": 49.01,
            "lon": 2.55
          },
          "zoom": 6,
          "label": "25 July 2000"
        }
      },
      "sources": [
        "https://www.businessinsurance.com/concorde-disaster-blamed-on-debris-from-other-jet/",
        "https://www.airport-technology.com/projects/concorde2/"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b18",
      "narration": "On the runway lay a strip of metal that had fallen from another airliner minutes earlier. Concorde's tyre hit it and burst. Debris struck the wing, a fuel tank ruptured, and the leaking fuel caught fire.",
      "visual": {
        "template": "list",
        "props": {
          "title": "A chain of failures",
          "items": [
            "A metal strip lies on the runway",
            "A tyre bursts",
            "Debris hits the wing, a tank ruptures",
            "The fuel catches fire"
          ]
        }
      },
      "sources": [
        "https://www.businessinsurance.com/concorde-disaster-blamed-on-debris-from-other-jet/",
        "https://www.airport-technology.com/projects/concorde2/"
      ],
      "cues": [
        "strip of metal",
        "burst",
        "Debris",
        "caught fire"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b19",
      "narration": "Less than two minutes after take-off, the aircraft crashed into a hotel in Gonesse. All one hundred and nine people on board and four people on the ground were killed.",
      "visual": {
        "template": "stat",
        "props": {
          "value": 113,
          "label": "Lives lost",
          "note": "109 on board and 4 on the ground"
        }
      },
      "sources": [
        "https://www.businessinsurance.com/concorde-disaster-blamed-on-debris-from-other-jet/"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b20",
      "narration": "It was the only fatal accident in Concorde's history, but it destroyed the aircraft's reputation for perfect safety. The whole fleet was grounded for more than a year.",
      "visual": {
        "template": "kinetic",
        "props": {
          "lines": [
            "Concorde's first",
            "and only fatal crash"
          ],
          "emphasis": [
            "only"
          ]
        }
      },
      "sources": [
        "https://www.airport-technology.com/projects/concorde2/",
        "https://us.cnn.com/2001/WORLD/europe/10/29/france.concorde/index.html"
      ],
      "cues": [
        "only fatal accident",
        "only fatal accident"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b21",
      "narration": "Engineers lined the fuel tanks with Kevlar and rubber, fitted tougher Michelin tyres and protected the wiring in the landing gear bay. Concorde was cleared to fly again.",
      "visual": {
        "template": "list",
        "props": {
          "title": "The fixes",
          "items": [
            "Kevlar-rubber fuel tank liners",
            "Burst-resistant Michelin tyres",
            "Protected wiring in the gear bay"
          ]
        }
      },
      "sources": [
        "https://www.heritageconcorde.com/caa-and-dgac-modifications-2001",
        "https://www.airport-technology.com/projects/concorde2/"
      ],
      "cues": [
        "Kevlar",
        "Michelin",
        "wiring"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b22",
      "narration": "It returned to service on the seventh of November, two thousand and one, just eight weeks after the September eleventh attacks had plunged air travel into a deep slump.",
      "visual": {
        "template": "timeline",
        "props": {
          "title": "Back at the worst moment",
          "highlight": 2,
          "events": [
            {
              "year": "Jul 2000",
              "label": "Flight 4590 crashes"
            },
            {
              "year": "Sep 2001",
              "label": "September 11 attacks"
            },
            {
              "year": "Nov 2001",
              "label": "Concorde returns"
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      "sources": [
        "https://us.cnn.com/2001/WORLD/europe/10/29/france.concorde/index.html",
        "https://www.insideflyer.com/posts/9-11-dealt-fatal-blow-to-concorde/"
      ],
      "cues": [
        "It returned",
        "September eleventh",
        "seventh of November"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b23",
      "narration": "Premium travellers were slow to come back, and the aircraft were getting old. Airbus, which supported the fleet, said keeping them flying would need costly extra maintenance.",
      "visual": {
        "template": "kinetic",
        "props": {
          "lines": [
            "Fewer passengers.",
            "Ageing aircraft.",
            "Rising costs."
          ],
          "emphasis": [
            "Rising"
          ]
        }
      },
      "sources": [
        "https://www.heritageconcorde.com/concorde-retirement-2003",
        "https://www.insideflyer.com/posts/9-11-dealt-fatal-blow-to-concorde/"
      ],
      "cues": [
        "slow to come back",
        "getting old",
        "costly"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b24",
      "narration": "In April two thousand and three, Air France and British Airways announced that Concorde would retire. The last commercial flight landed at Heathrow on the twenty-fourth of October.",
      "visual": {
        "template": "timeline",
        "props": {
          "title": "Concorde's life",
          "highlight": 3,
          "events": [
            {
              "year": "1969",
              "label": "First flight"
            },
            {
              "year": "1976",
              "label": "Passenger service"
            },
            {
              "year": "2000",
              "label": "Paris crash"
            },
            {
              "year": "2003",
              "label": "Retired"
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      "sources": [
        "https://www.heritageconcorde.com/concorde-retirement-2003",
        "https://www.history.com/this-day-in-history/october-24/the-concorde-makes-its-final-flight"
      ],
      "cues": [
        "In April",
        "In April",
        "In April",
        "would retire"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b25",
      "narration": "Concorde did not disappear because it was too fast. It disappeared because speed alone could not pay for a tiny, ageing fleet that burned so much fuel for every passenger.",
      "visual": {
        "template": "kinetic",
        "props": {
          "lines": [
            "Speed alone",
            "couldn't pay the bills."
          ],
          "emphasis": [
            "Speed"
          ]
        }
      },
      "sources": [
        "https://www.pbs.org/wgbh/nova/teachers/activities/3203_concorde.html",
        "https://www.heritageconcorde.com/concorde-retirement-2003"
      ],
      "cues": [
        "speed alone",
        "could not pay"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b26",
      "narration": "Since that last flight, no airliner has carried passengers faster than the speed of sound.",
      "visual": {
        "template": "fact",
        "props": {
          "kicker": "Since 24 October 2003",
          "text": "No airliner has carried passengers faster than the speed of sound."
        }
      },
      "sources": [
        "https://www.heritageconcorde.com/concorde-retirement-2003",
        "https://www.history.com/this-day-in-history/october-24/the-concorde-makes-its-final-flight"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b27",
      "narration": "But in twenty twenty-six, American regulators proposed lifting their old ban on supersonic flight over land. The question Concorde left behind is open again.",
      "visual": {
        "template": "title",
        "props": {
          "title": "Supersonic, Again?",
          "kicker": "What comes next",
          "subtitle": "In 2026 the FAA proposed lifting its ban on supersonic flight over land"
        }
      },
      "sources": [
